0342: SapphireGpu: pool buffers a client closes are kept and handed out again (kern.sweet_gpu_bocache, default 0)

Needs 0336. No change of behaviour unless switched on.

Measured with 0336 (15 minutes): 6575 GEM_NEW and 6670 frees; 1.44 s zeroing, 0.79 s mapping, 0.85 s
freeing. The Metal driver pools only its own slabs: every MTLBuffer and texture is a GEM_NEW and a
GEM_CLOSE.

kern.sweet_gpu_bocache (read-write, boot-arg gpu_bocache):
  0 (default)  nothing is kept;
  1  a pool buffer whose client closes it is kept, once the GPU is done with it, with its memory, GPU
     addresses and page table entries; a GEM_NEW of exactly that size by the same client gets it back under
     a new handle, zeroed again: no pool search, no mapping, no unmapping or TLB invalidation;
  2  as 1 without the zeroing: a client can find in a new buffer what an older buffer of its own held
     (never another client's). Metal promises zeroed buffers: for experiments only.
Limits: 32 buffers and 64 MB per client, none over 16 MB, each kept 2 s at most; all go back to the pool
when the client closes, and when the pool has no room for a new buffer.

kern.sweet_gpu_bocache_stats (read only): kept, handed out again (and bytes), freed for age, freed for room.

Locking: the kept buffers and counters are under service_lock, as the handle table is.

Measured (one boot, opening the app switcher twice, n=1): kept 282, handed out again 146 (92.6 MB); the
time of the first opening's slowest frame did not improve (470 ms against 228 ms without it).

